+## October 16, 2026: nature QA, where two things meet
+
+A pass over everything in nature where two things meet (outside the water and shore, and the night lights), engine-wide, from a harness that rings each spot with captures at eye level and from low down, before and after (island village and wood, San Ramon, the Diablo foothills, Muir Woods, Svalbard, the Sahara, the Alps, Kyoto, MAGMA, TERRAN, ICE). Fixed at the source: the grass's sun glow was unshadowed and the parting of the grass pulled tips toward the lens, so in a wood's shade or from a low or flying eye the turf showed pale stretched sheets and spikes, the owner's iPhone capture (`world/grass.js`: glow after the lights times the sun's shadow and the canopy, no tuft at the lens, parting only at a walker's eye height); leaves seen from behind went frosted white (`world/vegetation.js`: no specular on back faces); wild grass, ferns, litter and stones stood on asphalt and in car parks (`nature/wildground.js onPaving`); the Sahara was a green scrub (the hot desert thins with rain, keeps to the washes under 200 mm); the globe's ground speckled on every slope (its photo relief was 12 cm, now 1.5 cm, its grain halved); snow was a glossy wet floor (`world/terrain.js`: matte, glossy only on scoured ice); wild rocks had no foot (`nature/rocks.js`: a damp dark band where they meet the soil); trunks floated on the downhill side of slopes (`world/groundsnap.js`, new, used by `region/flora.js`, `earth/globetrees.js`, `world/vegetation.js`; low plants lie with the slope); the Bay's trees ignored the roads' grading (`bay/berms.js roadDelta`, `bay/city.js`, `bay/streettrees.js`); the globe's snow line followed contours (now by aspect, with scree below the crags and moss on shaded wet rock); every tree leaned on a gust at once (`GUST_GLSL`: the grass's own gust field); the sunbeams' hard edge at the shadow box (`world/sunrays.js`). Hardened: every GPU-lifted ground's height through `SAFE_GLSL` (bit-tested NaN and infinity, clamped), the berms' grids never carry NaN. Left: a bare brown building box in San Ramon, the dry-season Bay ground's flat near detail, no shadows past 55 m, the Bay and globe seam untested at eye level, Kyoto's town not captured. Tests: `island/tools/nature-qa.test.mjs`. See `docs/nature-qa-2026-10-16.md`.
+
 ## October 15, 2026: mobile controls
 
 The touch HUD has one look and one layout in portrait and landscape: round, semi-transparent buttons with a white line glyph and no words under or inside them (`island/src/ui/touch-skin.js`, applied once from `main.js` on touch screens only). Glyphs are inline SVG masks matched to each button by its name (anything unknown gets a pointing-hand glyph, never words; changing captions such as Takedown, Swing or Slash are glyph swaps); every button keeps its `aria-label` (one without is named from its words) and a title. By thumb, inside the safe areas: the primary action bottom right (Fire, Strike, the pedal, the cast) at 68 px with Aim/Block/brake, Reload/Dodge/a vehicle's gun, Mode/Grab and Surrender round it at 52 px; jump and the context buttons (leave the car, the boat, the rod, which car, assist, camera) in a column above; the stick, or steering (and an aircraft's Up and Down), bottom left; things to act on nearby in a row between the thumbs; back, home and the folded contract top left; fly, gear, places, the menu, music and settings in one row top right; tips under the top row. Driving, melee, boats and fishing only show their own controls; the drive button comes out of the menu as an exit while you drive. The quest chip has its own band and nothing sits on it (measured at run time, with Safari's bars read from the visual viewport). Looking round (`ui/touch-look.js`): a floating stick in the lower left, free look anywhere else with both thumbs at once, an acceleration curve, invert up and down and gyro aiming in Sky and World, and the view easing back to level as you walk. Control tips on a phone show once (`firstTime`). Desktop unchanged apart from tooltips. Tests: `island/tools/touch-skin.test.mjs`, `island/tools/mobile-controls-browser.cjs` (now with the skin). See `docs/mobile-controls-2026-10-15.md`.
